      <div type="commentary">
        <p n="1">The framed manuscript page number [ⲓⲃ] is printed with a bar above and below as the frame, not as a supralinear stroke.</p>
        <p n="4">The edition prints an opening bracket before ⲗⲏⲥⲓⲁ and no closing bracket; the restoration and the five lost letters after it are taken to end before ⲩⲉ.</p>
        <p n="12">The editors expect [ⲛⲱϩⲉ] at the end of the line (apparatus).</p>
        <p n="13">The editors print that this line was not read; no text is given.</p>
        <p n="15">The line ends with an opening bracket and nothing after it; the bracket is closed only in l. 16, after seven dots. Here it is kept as a transcriber note at its place; l. 16 reopens the bracket.</p>
        <p n="16">The edition prints the closing bracket after seven dots with no opening bracket on this line (it was opened at the end of l. 15).</p>
        <p n="19">The overline over ⲓⲏⲥ is printed high, between ll. 18 and 19; measured, it covers ⲏⲥ and only a third of ⲓ (bar 1175–1209, ⲓ 1171–1176).</p>
        <p n="20">The overline of [ⲡⲭⲣⲥ] covers ⲭⲣⲥ and not ⲡ (measured), as in l. 21.</p>
        <p n="22">Measured overline 621–667: it covers ⲛ and ⲓ fully, ⲕ by 57 % and ⲡ by 46 %, so it is encoded on ⲛⲓⲕ. In ϩⲛ̄ at the start of the line the stroke covers ⲛ (56 %) but not ϩ (43 %).</p>
        <p n="25">The final ⲛ is printed blurred (heavy ink) in the scan.</p>
        <p n="29">⟨ⲥ⟩ is the editors' correction (apparatus: at the end read ⲛⲉϩ⟨ⲥ⟩ⲉ).</p>
        <p n="32">The overline over ⲓⲏⲥ starts at ⲓ (measured); ⲓ also carries a sublinear dot.</p>
        <p n="34">The edition opens a bracket before ⲓ and does not close it; a wide unmarked blank follows, then the bracketed [ⲁ ⲛ̄ⲓ̈ⲟⲩⲇⲁⲓ]. The first restoration is closed after ⲓ here.</p>
      </div>
